export const READABLE_SCANNED_TRIAL_PARTS: Record<string,Record<string,string>> = {
  "1C9pC0gKe7AuJVfXqWA4CNpzX50nCKAo_": {
    "1": "Part 1\nQuestions 1 to 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the\ncorrect answer A, B or C.\nYou will hear each recording twice. Answer all questions.\n1. What benefit does the speaker mention about spending time with his extended family?\nA. It keeps them close and helps them understand each other.\nB. It gives them more chances to celebrate together.\nC. It helps them deal with family problems better.\n2. What is the speaker's main concern about leaving Facebook?\nA. She will no longer be able to communicate with her friends.\nB. She will take too much of her time to save the old photos.\nC. She will lose access to the images she uploaded.\n3. Based on Lily's adventures, which word best describes the places she has been?\nA. Dangerous\nB. Colourful\nC. Beautiful\n\n4. Based on Danial's advice, what should Amir do?\nA. Keep some of the money as savings before buying anything.\nB. Spend half of the money now since the phone is on sale.\nC. Use part of the money to treat his friends at the café.\n5. The main difference between the weather patterns in Klang Valley and London is ...\nA. the temperature of the climate.\nB. the number of thunderstorm days.\nC. the conditions for thunderstorms to form.\n6. What did the authorities encourage families and schools to do?\nA. Report all teenage crimes directly to the police immediately.\nB. Work together to guide teenagers and help prevent crime.\nC. Remind everyone to maintain safety and harmony.\n7. Based on the statement, the art student realised that ...\nA. traditional crafts are more valuable than modern art.\nB. the latest fashion trends made him uncomfortable.\nC. modern art helped him in accepting himself.",
    "2": "Part 2\nQuestions 8 to 15\nYou will listen to Mira, talking about her obsession. For questions 8 to 15, choose the best\nanswer A, B or C.\n8. Why is Mira not ready to fix her shopping addiction?\nA. Shopping is her only way to have fun.\nB. Shopping gives her more confidence.\nC. Shopping is a difficult habit to break.\n9. What did Mira discover that she thinks is worrying?\nA. Her never-ending childhood wish list\nB. Her appreciation of the new clothes scent\nC. Her parents' generosity during her childhood\n10. What is the typical gift Mira received from her family?\nA. Money.\nB. Dresses.\nC. Giftcards.\n11. What does Mira find most dangerous about her online shopping habit?\nA. She never leaves her bed to shop.\nB. She fears getting the wrong size item.\nC. She has noticed her bank account is unstable.\n\n12. Which almost made Mira fix her obsession?\nA. Deleting shopping apps.\nB. Creating a stricter budget.\nC. Talking herself out of shopping.\n13. Mira views her daily fashion choices as a way for her to ...\nA. express happiness.\nB. share her life stories.\nC. impress other people.\n14. Mira prefers when the world sees her as...\nA. a classy lady in a dress and heels.\nB. a confident CEO in a blazer and flats.\nC. a relaxed friend in an oversized hoodie.\n15. How does Mira feel about her shopping habit?\nA. She is not ready to fix it.\nB. She makes excuses for it.\nC. She thinks it is not a problem.",
    "3": "Part 3\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five speakers share their experiences during a difficult mountain expedition.\nFor questions 16 to 20, choose from the list A to G what each speaker says. Use the letters\nonly once. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all questions.\nA It is best to get on with the climb only when the weather gets better.\nB It is important to save your supplies when you are trapped in a valley.\nC You must not panic even if you are injured during the trek.\nD Being an explorer in the valley requires more strength than climbing.\nE Staying with a companion helps you cope with difficult moments.\nF Finding a path to the peak is impossible when you are exhausted.\nG An explorer needs mental strength to survive a long ordeal.\n16. Speaker 1\n17. Speaker 2\n18. Speaker 3\n19. Speaker 4\n20. Speaker 5",
    "4": "Part 4\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear an interview about Malaysian superstitions and why it is important to know about\nthem. For questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space.\nUse NO MORE THAN ONE WORD OR A NUMBER for each space.\nYou will listen to the interview twice. Answer all questions.\nMalaysian Superstitions in the Forests\nAliff works as a forest (21) ______ in a large rainforest. Many people visit\nthe forest because of the scenery. However, some visitors believe the forest is also mysterious\nand fascinating, which can lead to some weird (22) ______ Aliff explains that the forest is usually peaceful, but it is important to (23) ______ the forest spirits. One day, while guiding hikers, a woman in the group said\nshe heard someone whispering her name. There was no one nearby. Aliff advised the group\nto remain (24) ______ and not reply to the voice. According to local belief, voices in\nthe jungle should not be answered because they may be dangerous.\nAliff also shared his experience during a search and rescue operation. A young man\nwent missing after taking a (25) ______ hike alone. He later said he met a woman\nwho showed him a shortcut. Rescuers found him deep in the forest, (26) ______ and\nafraid. However, no one can say for sure if the woman was a supernatural being or just the\nhiker's (27) ______ When asked about how the rescue team found the missing hiker, Aliff explained that\nthey searched near rivers and marked trails. They also did not shout loudly, which some\nrescuers believe helps. Hours passed, and they were (28) ______  when they found\nhim inside a cave, weak but alive.\nThis experience teaches hikers a few important lessons. To begin with, safety comes\nfirst. Then, hikers should always respect nature by being careful and respectful of local (29) ______ Lastly, Aliffs reminds everyone that although nature is (30) ______ , we should not treat it as a playground."
  },
  "1dVrohrk3TxpLfV68RoSaSTKttDTu5Ddm": {
    "1": "Part 1\nQuestions 1 to 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the\ncorrect answer (A, B or C). You will hear each recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n1. What should students bring?\nA. Food and drinks.\nB. Bottles and plastic bags.\nC. Face masks and newspapers.\n2. What changed the writer's opinion about reading?\nA. After developing writing skills.\nB. After discovering interesting stories.\nC. After attending the reading workshop.\n3. Where will Amir and Sarah most likely go to print the pictures?\nA. The school library.\nB. The computer shop.\nC. The shop near the bus station.\n4. When did cycling start to feel easier and more enjoyable?\nA. Last year\nB. Last month\nC. Last two weeks\n5. How did the singer become successful?\nA. She wrote many songs.\nB. She went on a world tour.\nC. She won a music competition.\n6. Which group of people can still go out tomorrow despite the weather conditions?\nA. Drivers\nB. Farmers\nC. Fishermen\n7. Which phone is considered the most practical for students?\nA. Nothing Phone\nB. Galaxy S Series\nC. Moto Flip Phone\n\nSULIT 3 1119/4",
    "2": "Part 2\nQuestions 8 to 15\nYou will hear an article about some of South Korea's most famous tourism spots. For questions\n8 to 15, circle the correct answer (A, B or C). You will hear the recording twice. Answer all\nquestions.\n8. What can South Korea offer to visitors?\nA. A variety of food and entertainment.\nB. The famous K-Pop entertainment industry.\nC. Cities, history, and beautiful natural scenery.\n9. Why is Seoul often the first destination for tourists?\nA. It is lively and dynamic with entertainment.\nB. Most famous landmarks are located in the city.\nC. It offers a mix of modernness and tradition in one place.\n10. Where can visitors experience a glimpse of life in the previous eras?\nA. Buckchon Hanok Village.\nB. Gyeongbokgung Palace.\nC. N Seoul Tower.\n11. What makes Jeongbang Waterfall different from many other waterfalls in Asia?\nA. It is known for its extreme height and strong flow towards the land.\nB. It is located deep within a volcanic lava tube formation.\nC. It is one of the few waterfalls that flow into the sea.\n12. Why is Hallasan Mountain suitable for many types of hikers?\nA. It offers trails that are designed only for expert climbers.\nB. It has hiking paths with different levels of challenges.\nC. It provides guided tours exclusively for beginners.\n13. What is Haeundae Beach NOT mainly known for?\nA. Camping activities\nB. Release stress\nC. Swimming\n14. Why is Gyeongju important to visitors?\nA. It is a city known for its advanced technology.\nB. It is a former capital rich with heritage sites.\nC. The place is a modern kingdom.\n15. What is the main advantage of the KTX high-speed train in South Korea?\nA. It enables journeys to be completed within hours.\nB. It connects rural villages with limited transport options.\nC. It is mainly used for short-distance travel within a single city.\n\nSULIT 4 1119/4",
    "3": "Part 3\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five short extracts in which people talk about types of shopping places. For\nquestions 16 to 20, choose from the list (A-G) what each speaker says about their preparation.\nUse the letters only once. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use. You will\nhear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\nA. Many choices can be easily viewed online, and lower prices are often found during\npromotions.\nB. Time with friends can be enjoyable and different options can be explored at shopping\ncentres.\nC. Night markets are chosen for its calm atmosphere, with fixed rates applied.\nD. Well-made items are chosen for its long-lasting quality, and purchases are made\ncalmly during quiet times.\nE. Stylish items are found at lower prices, and a lively setting is enjoyed.\nF. Rare outfits are discovered at low cost, and the search is enjoyed like a game.\nG. Shopping at branded stores is avoided due to fewer options and higher prices, while\nfeedback is overlooked.\nSpeaker 1 16.\nSpeaker 2 17.\nSpeaker 3 18.\nSpeaker 4 19.\nSpeaker 5 20.\n\nSULIT 5 1119/4",
    "4": "Part 4\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear a reporter interviewing a scam victim about her experience. For questions 21 to\n30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space. Use NO MORE THAN ONE\nWORD for each space. You will hear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\nA Costly Lesson: A Victim's Experience with Online Scamming\nOnline scamming has become a serious problem today, affecting many people no\nmatter what their background is. One victim shared her experience of being tricked by a\ncaller who claimed to be from a bank. The man sounded so (21) ______  _, and he even\nknew her personal details, which made her trust him at first. He warned her about\n(22) ______ activity on her bank account and said someone had tried to withdraw\nmoney. This caused her to panic, and the caller used that fear to control the situation.\nHe insisted that she needed to act quickly to (23) ______  her account or risk\nlosing everything. He then asked her to transfer her money to a so-called 'safe account' as a\n(24) ______ step. Believing it was just a way to ensure (25) ______  _, she\nfollowed his instructions without questioning them. At that moment, she was only focused\non protecting her (26) ______  . However, once the transaction was completed, the\ncaller suddenly (27) ______  She felt shocked and later blamed herself for being\ncareless. After knowing it was a scam, she immediately contacted her bank and made a police\n(28) ______ , but the money was already gone.\nLooking back, she noticed clear warning signs such as pressure and urgency. She now\nadvises others to make sure they (29) ______ the identity of the person calling and\navoid making decisions under emotional pressure. Next, she also reminds people never to\ntrust unknown callers or transfer money to unfamiliar (30) ______  - The reporter\nthanks Melissa for sharing her story, as raising awareness is the best way to prevent such\nscams."
  },
  "13phbSk03wJy80ZzMCDJRcr6qOO5gyWi2": {
    "1": "Part 1 (SET 3)\nQuestions 1 to 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the correct\nanswer (A,B, or C).\nYou will hear each recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n1 What did the speaker enjoy most about the festival?\nA People of various cultures celebrating together.\nB Various traditional foods sold at many stalls.\nC Watching traditional cultural performances.\n2 What changed after the campaign was explained to students?\nA Students started recycling campaign.\nB Students separated their rubbish properly.\nC Students put recycling bins around the school.\n3 Why did the speaker decide not to buy most of the items?\nA There was limited discounts.\nB There were unnecessary items in the cart.\nC There was an advice to spend on important things.\n4 What is the main goal of the exchange programme?\nA To learn different cultures.\nB To share various cultural practices.\nC To exchange different foods and routines.\n5 How do we make crowded cities more self-sufficient?\nA Solving food-shortage.\nB Saving water resources.\nC Growing crops in stacks.\n6 Why is this backpack a good choice for students?\nA The backpack is durable.\nB The backpack carries heavy books.\nC The backpack is popular among students.\n7 What activities will he be doing during the holiday?\nA Camping and exploring a national park.\nB Hiking and picniking at the waterfalls.\nC Checking out wildlife at the park.",
    "2": "Part 2\nQuestions 8 to 15\nYou will hear a radio presenter, Sarah, is hosting a cultural documentary segment on a local\ncommunity radio station talking about the comeback of traditional weaving. For questions 8 to 15,\nchoose the correct answer (A, B or C).\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n8 What did the patterns originally represent?\nA Stories on family backgrounds and spiritual beliefs.\nB Celebrations of family lineage and local history.\nC Daily chores and connections to nature.\n9 What was the main reason weaving almost disappeared?\nA Ready-made clothes grew cheaper and more popular.\nB. The elders wanted to teach their skills to the youth.\nC. Young people started working in the cities.\n10 How does the speaker feel about youth learning weaving?\nA Confident about their ability to master the skill.\nB Proud that they can make a business out of it.\nC Deeply moved by their eagerness to learn.\n11 What does the word motifs mean?\nA Decorative weaving designs for business.\nB Decorative designs or patterns used in art.\nC Decorative materials used to make clothing.\n12 How do youth make weaving attractive to international buyers?\nA Printing cultural designs onto modern fashion.\nB Combining tribal patterns with modern clothing.\nC Selling contemporary clothing to foreign tourists.\n13 Why do beginners feel difficult to learn weaving?\nA Spending more than three months on their very first try.\nB Memorising complex geometric patterns by heart.\nC Collecting plants from the jungle to create dyes.\n14 What is the non-financial benefit of practising weaving?\nA Feeling mentally calmed from their busy digital lives.\nB Feeling recognised for preserving cultural traditions.\nC Feeling connected to their past and cultural identity.\n\n15 What is the main message?\nA Traditional weaving has deep cultural meaning.\nB Traditional weaving is a good business in modern society.\nC Traditional weaving should be practised by rural communities.",
    "3": "Part 3\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five short extracts about smart spending habits\nFor questions 16 to 20, choose from the list A to G what each speaker says. Use the letters only once.\nThere are two extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou will hear each recording twice. Answer all the questions.\nA Evaluating options promotes responsible spending.\nSpeaker 1 16\nB. Saving the right way keeps you secure.\nSpeaker 2 17\nC. Increasing earnings for increased personal spending.\nSpeaker 3 18\nD. Earning and learning through helping others.\nSpeaker 4 19\nE Earning from what you love and managing it well.\nSpeaker 5 20\nF Regular saving provides essential financial security.\nG Investing money with higher financial returns.",
    "4": "Part 4\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear an interview talking about environmental health.\nFor questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space.\nUse no more than one word for each space.\nYou will hear the recording twice.\nENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH AND US\nEnvironmental health is about how our environment affects both our body and mental well-\nbeing. It includes the air (21) ______  → the water we drink, the food we eat and the places where\nwe live, study and relax. When these (22) ______ are safe, people will usually have better health.\nHowever, polluted air can cause health problems. High pollution levels make breathing even harder\nfor those with lung problems. In cities, heavy (23) ______ and factories can release smoke and\nchemicals into the air.\nWater safety is also important. In the event of water contamination, people may get stomach\n(24) ______ . Sadly, not many people realise that certain (25) ______ around the world face critical\naccess to clean water. Environmental health is not only about physical health. It also affects how\npeople feel emotionally. Spending time in natural places with lots of greeneries can help reduce\nanxiety and (26) ______ : Although technology is useful, teenagers should balance between\nspending time on screens and outdoors. Low-impact physical activities, such as walking and\ngardening provide (27) ______ for their overall health.\nThrough simple habitual practices, teenagers can help preserve the environment. For example,\nthey can reduce plastic use as part of supporting (28) ______ campaigns. They can also walk or\ncycle for short journeys instead of taking the bus or car. These actions can lower levels of pollution\nand encourage a healthier lifestyle. Dr Farid believes that environmental (29) ______ and\npersonal well-being are interrelated. By conserving nature, they are also (30) ______ their own\nwell-being and others. He also says that teenagers possess the power to safeguard their families,\nfriends and communities through their choices."
  },
  "1855WZmAXmzZ6_LMzwdSbuuZfDaBX9yef": {
    "1": "Part 1\n[7 marks]\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the correct\nanswer A, B or C. You will hear each recording twice. Answer all questions.\n1 A good way for students to save money is by ...\nA buying second-hand books from the library.\nB having takeout meals with friends.\nC keeping track of their spending.\n2 What does the student think about his new skill?\nA It helped him fix broken laptops.\nB It helped him gain self-confidence.\nC It helped him to create the school website\n3 Students should take care of their mental health by ...\nA maintaining a routine.\nB focusing on being helpful.\nC expressing gratitude to others.\n4 Celebrating Hari Raya with the friend made the speaker value ...\nA the close friendship.\nB the different cultures.\nC the interesting stories.\n5 What is the speaker's opinion about computer games?\nA They are better than sports.\nB They are completely harmful.\nC They are useful if balanced well.\n6 When the princess set seven impossible tasks for the Sultan, she wanted to ...\nA prove her power.\nB show her courage.\nC refuse his proposal.\n7 What benefit does the speaker get from this daily routine?\nA Staying informed about international events.\nB Discussing current issues online.\nC Watching short videos.\n\nSULIT 3 1119/4",
    "2": "Part 2\n[8 marks]\nYou will hear a health officer talking about the importance of taking care of one's health as a\nteenager. For questions 8 to 15, choose the correct answer A, B or C. You will hear each recording\ntwice. Answer all questions.\n8 Why is it important for teenagers to take care of their health?\nA They are stressed and confused all the time.\nB They are facing challenging moments in their lives.\nC They are facing both physical and emotional changes quickly.\n9 To be active healthy teenagers, they should get enough sleep and ...\nA do physical activities.\nB focus better in school.\nC go to the gym and exercise.\n10 What must a teenager ensure when it comes to food?\nA They have to make sure to eat more fruits and protein.\nB They need to eat proteins such as beans or chicken.\nC They must make sure to not miss eating breakfast.\n11 What are some of the things that teenagers should avoid?\nA Eating fast food and sweet snacks.\nB Going on a wholesome diet.\nC Eating unprocessed foods.\n12 A well balanced diet ...\nA is about eating only rice or pasta.\nB is a wholesome way to be perfect.\nC is needed in the process of growing.\n13 What should teenagers learn to do?\nA They must take school very seriously.\nB They must be aware of what they feel.\nC They must focus only on physical health.\n14 What is recommended to care for your mental health?\nA Care for someone who has problems.\nB Talk to someone trusted that calms you.\nC Make it a habit to help a family member.\n15 A smart teenager knows ...\nA how to show his feelings.\nB a counsellor should be a professional.\nC when to ask for help from a professional.\n\nSULIT 4 1119/4",
    "3": "Part 3\n[5 marks]\nYou will hear five short extracts in which people are giving their opinion on the criteria to purchase\na smartphone.\nFor questions 16 - 20, choose from the list (A - G) what each speaker says. Use the letters only\nonce. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use. You will hear each recording\ntwice. Answer all questions.\nA The speaker says that users consider fast-charging as the\nmain criteria in buying a smart phone. Speaker 1 16\nB The speaker feels that a fast processor, sufficient storage Speaker 2 17\nand smooth multitasking matter more than stylish\nfeatures. Speaker 3 18\nC The speaker feels that long battery life allows users to stay Speaker 4 19\nconnected throughout the day without constantly\nsearching for a charger. Speaker 5 20\nD The speaker feels that a good smartphone does not have\nto be expensive if it meets basic needs well.\nE The speaker thinks that buying the latest model helps users\nget the best value for their money.\nF The speaker feels that features such as image stabilisation,\nnight mode and video quality matter more than how fast it\nis.\nG The speaker thinks that a comfortable grip, attractive\nappearance and good interface improve users' daily\nusage.\n\nSULIT 5 1119/4",
    "4": "Part 4\n[10 marks]\nYou will hear a conversation between friends talking about the effects of ice melting in the Arctic\nand Antarctica.\nFor questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space.\nUse NO MORE THAN ONE WORD for each space. You will hear the conversation twice.\nAnswer all questions.\nWhen the ice melts, everyone feels it\nThe main parts of the polar ice caps, the Arctic and Antarctica are (21) ______ quickly\nbecause of temperatures that are rising, especially in the summer season. The melting ice affects\nanimals like polar bears and (22) ______ because they need ice to hunt and rest. If the ice\nkeeps disappearing, they will lose their homes and some species may even (23) ______ \nfrom those regions. The effects are not limited to wildlife; indigenous communities are also affected\nbecause their homes and (24) ______  depend on the ice, and melting ice can damage\nhouses and roads and make hunting harder. Even though, no one lives in Antarctica, the ice there\nhelps to control (25) ______ sea levels. When the sea levels rise everywhere as the ice\nmelts, it will lead to flooding of cities near the coast. Melting ice also changes ocean\n(26) ______ which help control weather. This can lead to stronger storms, more heatwaves,\nserious floods and long (27) ______ in certain parts of the world. Ice also normally reflects\nsunlight, but when it melts, the dark ocean absorbs more heat, creating a (28) ______ loop\nthat makes the planet warm faster. To reduce the impact, people should reduce greenhouse gas\n(29) ______ and use renewable energy like solar and wind. Individuals can save energy at\nhome, use public transport and make small (30) ______ changes. Communities and schools\ncan start an eco-club, organise tree planting and raise awareness. We should all play a part no\nmatter how small it is for the sake of our future."
  },
  "10-xHI23J_dJC7zaxkYZUpPoesBTxcK66": {
    "1": "Part 1\n(7 marks]\nAnswer all the questions.\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the\ncorrect answer (A, B, or C).\nYou will hear each recording twice.\n1. At the Eastern Valley Mall Great Sale, visitors can\nA get 50% discounts on all items.\nB enjoy unlimited promotions.\nC watch live performances.\n2. From the news, we know that Diana Ariana\nA has an injury on her wrist\nB will compete on Saturday.\nC is the runner-up for the Thailand Open.\n3. What advice does Aina give Ravi about packing?\nA Pack some snacks for the trip\nB Bring extra clothes and shoes\nC Save some space for gifts to bring home\n4. What must students do before submitting their recyclable items?\nA Make sure the items are not dirty or wet\nB Collect and send them individually\nC Sort the items by colour\n5. After her first travel experience, she has learnt to be\nA calm even when alone.\nB nice to everyone.\nC independent.\n\n6. From the announcement, we know that\nA the key is very special to the owner.\nB the key must be handed over to the owner.\nC the person who found the key will be rewarded.\n7. Both of them agreed that\nA the players' safety is important.\nB the players are passionate.\nC the game is thrilling.",
    "2": "Part 2\n(8 marks]\nAnswer all the questions.\nYou will hear Izzul sharing his experience helping a friend in studying.\nFor questions 8 to 15, circle the correct answer (A, B, or C).\nYou will hear the recording twice.\n8. What was Amir's main problem in learning English?\nA He could not write complex sentences.\nB He was not interested in writing English essays.\nC Hehas difficulty with text comprehension and writing.\n9. Why did Izzul decide to help Amir?\nA He wanted to improve Amir's confidence.\nB He noticed the upset and the great attempt.\nC He was interested in helping the teacher.\n10. What was Izzul's first step in helping Amir?\nA Identifying his learning difficulties\nB Providing him with a model essay writing\nC Encouraging memorisation of the words\n11. Izzul's suggestion on writing short paragraphs helps\nA building ideas slowly.\nB preparing for a full essay.\nC making the essays interesting.\n12. How did the study routine affect Amir?\nA It consumed his time.\nB It improved his grammar.\nC It increased his confidence.\n\n13. Izzul's past experience helped Amir to\nA replace the need for practice.\nB make learning easier.\nC stay motivated\n14. What did Amir's gradual improvement suggest about learning?\nA It took time, effort, and consistency.\nB It happened with the right mentor.\nC It required natural talent only.\n15. What was the most important lesson Izzul learned?\nA He learned to be patient.\nB He achieved success too.\nC He valued their friendship.",
    "3": "Part 3\n(5 marks]\nAnswer all the questions.\nYou will hear five short extracts in which people are talking about things they consider\nwhen choosing a travel destination. For questions 16 to 20, choose from the list (A to G)\nwhat each speaker says. Use the letters only once. There are two extra letters which you do\nnot need to use.\nYou will hear the recording twice.\nA researches safety before travelling\nSpeaker 1 16\nB checks the weather before travelling\nC selects budget-friendly activities Speaker 2 17\nD is attracted to local traditions Speaker 3 18\nE loves nature and outdoor activities Speaker 4 19\nF enjoys outdoor water activities Speaker 5 20\nG chooses a destination that suits the budget",
    "4": "Part 4\n(10 marks]\nAnswer all the questions.\nQuestions 21 - 30\nYou will hear an interview with Dr Keera, an economist, who is talking about how to prepare\nfor an economic crisis. For questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each\nnumbered space.\nUse NO MORE THAN ONE WORD for each space.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\nBe Ready: A Youth's Guide to Economic Challenges\nEconomic crises are a normal part of the economic world. When it happens, the\nmost important step is to be (21) ______  It can be scary. However, people should not panic\nbut instead (22) ______  on what they can control.\nIt is important to build healthy financial (23) ______  early in life. For example,\nsaving money regularly, even in small amounts, can help create an emergency (24) ______ \nThis money can be useful during difficult times. In addition, young people should learn to\nmanage their spending by understanding the (25) ______  between a need and a want.\nStudents should develop beneficial (26) ______  such as communication,\nteamwork, and digital abilities. These can help them adapt more easily to changes in the work\n(27) ______  _ He believes that being flexible and willing to learn is very important.\nFurthermore, having more than one source of (28) ______  can provide extra\nsecurity. Young people can try simple side activities like tutoring or online work to earn extra\nmoney and gain experience.\nFinally, young people should stay (29) ______  and concentrate on self-\nimprovement. Although crises can be challenging, they can also bring endless (30) ______ \nWith the right mindset and preparation, young people can handle difficult situations more\nconfidently."
  },
  "1ExeSxH-hgiw1XanhfIZxLZT5Fmqj5J6M": {
    "1": "Part 1\nQuestions 1 to 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the\ncorrect answer (A, B or C).\nYou will hear each recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n1 What are the chores Billy volunteered for?\nA Washing dishes and sweeping\nB Taking out the trash and sweeping\nC Washing dishes and cleaning the toilet\n2 The classes\nA are free of charge.\nB can improve fitness.\nC are available online.\n3 The speaker suggests using the 30-day rule primarily to\nA find a cheaper alternative for the product.\nB ensure the product is still in stock after a month.\nC avoid making emotional and unplanned purchases.\n4 One benefit of using technology in education is\nA it improves research.\nB it makes learning more interactive.\nC it engages pupils' technological skills.\n5 What is the main purpose of the festival?\nA To promote modern art and music.\nB To attract tourists from other countries.\nC To celebrate and preserve traditional customs.\n6 Aina decided to start cycling to work to\nA reduce her monthly transportation expenses.\nB encourage Ben to join her in living sustainably.\nC save the environment and improve her mental health.\n7 When not playing video games, the boy\nA watches television.\nB does outdoor activities.\nC revises for his examinations.\n\nSULIT 3 1119/4",
    "2": "Part 2\nQuestions 8 to 15\nYou will hear Sarah, a lifestyle blogger, giving a talk about a zero-waste lifestyle. For questions\n8 to 15, choose the correct answer (A, B or C).\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all questions.\n8 What first motivated Sarah to start a zero-waste lifestyle?\nA To improve her physical well-being\nB A suggestion from her doctor to eat less salt\nC Her concern for the increasing ocean pollution\n9 How did Sarah's shopping habits change?\nA She began frequenting local markets.\nB She purchased lesser instant meals.\nC She started buying organic food.\n10 The positive side effect of Sarah cooking from scratch was\nA she saved more money on meal preparation.\nB she discovered new recipes from the local market.\nC she unintentionally reduced her intake of food additives.\n11 The most difficult part of Sarah's journey was\nA remembering to bring her own cloth bags.\nB dealing with reactions of people around her.\nC giving up her favourite snacks that come in plastic.\n12 How did Sarah's friends eventually react to her lifestyle?\nA They stopped inviting her to eat at cafés.\nB They were inspired to adopt some of her habits.\nC They continued to find her behaviour awkward.\n13 Regarding the cost of living sustainably, Sarah emphasises that\nA eco-friendly brands are worth the high price.\nB making your own cleaners is more expensive.\nC it is cheaper because it focuses on buying less.\n14 What is Sarah's advice for beginners?\nA Focus on replacing the item you discard most frequently.\nB Aim for a perfect zero-waste home within a month.\nC Replace plastic items with glass jars.\n15 What is the main message of Sarah's talk?\nA Individual perfection is a way to save the planet.\nB Home-cooking is the most effective way to save money.\nC Major change comes from many people making small efforts.",
    "3": "Part 3\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five short extracts in which people talk about how they try to be charitable. For\nquestions 16 to 20, choose from the list (A to G) what each speaker says. Use the letters only\nonce. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all questions.\nA I believe charity begins at home.\nB I spend my weekends helping children.\nC I will continue to help others when I\nSpeaker 1 16\npass on.\nD Speaker 2 17\nI help the elderly clean their house on\nweekends.\nSpeaker 3 18\nE I give 20 percent of my monthly salary\nto the poor. Speaker 4 19\nF I donate items that I have saved to Speaker 5 20\ninternational agencies.\nG I deliver information on how to help the\nunfortunate.\nCamScanner",
    "4": "Part 4\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear a student and a teacher, planning their year-end farewell party. For questions\n21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space. Use NO MORE THAN\nONE WORD for each space.\nYou will hear the interview twice. Answer all the questions.\nFarewell Party\nWith the budget of RM 1200, there is quite a bit they can do as long as they plan wisely.\nThis amount was achieved through fund-raising events as well as class (21) ______  The\nmain expense of course would be (22) ______  _ and beverages. Instead of catering, some\ndishes and snacks could be prepared together. This may not be the best option as students have\n(23) ______ to prepare for as well. Making our own (24) ______  might also save some money\nas well as add a personal touch. Everyone would look forward to some light entertainment but\nthat can be expensive so using our own songs and (25) ______  - might be the wiser option.\nAs a parting gift, students will appreciate some souvenirs. Instead of keychains,\npersonalised (26) ______  would be cheaper. We should always anticipate emergencies and\nkeep some money as (27) ______  _. Keeping receipts is also important. Just in case, at the end\nof the month, a financial (28) ______  should be prepared. It is best to update the whole class\nabout the expenses regularly so everyone knows how their money is being spent. Another\noption to raise extra money for the party is if we (29) ______ snacks. At the end of the day, we\nshould try to make this event a (30) ______  one to mark an end to a wonderful year."
  },
  "12wkTckGGtJRsCFH_-ojXYWLzyldevsg7": {
    "1": "Part 1\n[7 marks]\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the correct\nanswer (A, B or C). You will hear each recording twice.\n1. Authorities are advising people to ...\nA stay safe and be prepared.\nB post about further dangers.\nC go outside and help evacuate residents.\n2. The girl believes that ...\nA no one can challenge the hare in a race.\nB the tortoise is always proud of his abilities.\nC to win a race you have to maintain a steady pace.\n3. What is one problem mentioned in the passage?\nA Technology is clean energy.\nB Scientists create new inventions.\nC Computers taking over work from people.\n4. The main idea of the text is..\nA discounts and promotions help save money.\nB shopping during sales is always a good idea.\nC making a shopping list helps control spending.\n5. Which of these is a symbolic ritual?\nA Miring\nB Ngajat\nC Manok Pansoh\n6. Technology helps in.\nA preventing risks.\nB detecting diseases.\nC avoiding radiation effects.\n7. How do advertisements affect consumers?\nA They always provide honest product details.\nB They sometimes encourage impulse buying.\nC They stop people from buying unnecessary items.\n\nSULIT 3 1119/4",
    "2": "Part 2\n[8 marks]\nYou will hear a volunteer sharing her experience helping a flood affected village. For questions 8\nto 15, choose the correct answer (A, B or C). You will hear each recording twice.\n8. What motivated the volunteer to join the recovery efforts in Jambu Rias?\nA The government asked for her help.\nB The terrible images of the devastation.\nC The volunteer group that she decided to join.\n9. When she first arrived at the village, she saw the ...\nA victims asking for help.\nB floodwaters and mud were still rising.\nC overwhelming destruction caused by the flood.\n10. What was the first task assigned to her?\nA Distributing aid packages.\nB Restoring essential infrastructure.\nC Helping remove mud and wreckage.\n11. Meeting Puan Siti was touching for the writer because she ...\nA had lost everything in the flood\nB was still hopeful despite the difficulties.\nC has experience facing floods all her life.\n12. The medical volunteers mainly helped with ...\nA medical supplies.\nB medical treatment.\nC the temporary shelter.\n13. What additional support was provided for villagers needing intensive care?\nA Temporary shelter was set up for them.\nB The medical volunteers gave them treatment.\nC They were transported to the nearest hospital.\n14. What was the children's first reaction to the volunteers?\nA They were shy and withdrawn.\nB They warmed up to the volunteers.\nC They played games with the volunteers.\n15. At the end of her time in Jambu Rias, she learnt about ...\nA living in a community.\nB the journey to restoration.\nC the power of human kindness and strength.\n\nSULIT 4 1119/4",
    "3": "Part 3\n[5 marks]\nYou will hear five short extracts where people talk about ways to get news and information.\nFor questions 16 - 20, choose from the list (A - G) what each speaker says. Use the letters only\nonce. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use. You will hear each recording\ntwice. Answer all questions.\nA. The speaker prefers news from trusted sources\ndespite it being slower because it is checked by Speaker 1 16\nprofessionals.\nSpeaker 2 17\nB. The speaker thinks podcasts are like reading articles\non specific topics. Speaker 3 18\nC. The speaker feels that people should be wary about Speaker 4 19\nnews from social media because some news can be\nfake.\nSpeaker 5 20\nD. The speaker feels that TV and online news is the\nmost up-dated news because it is broadcasted at\nspecific times.\nE. The speaker feels that despite having wider access to\nnews on apps and websites, traditional news had\nmore depth and context.\nF. The speaker prefers listening to podcasts as they\nexplore various topics and are more in-depth.\nG. The speaker prefers getting her news from social\nmedia because it is updated and fast.\n\nSULIT 5 1119/4",
    "4": "Part 4\n[10 marks]\nYou will hear a conversation between a mother and a son talking about money and financial\nplanning. For questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space.\nUse NO MORE THAN ONE WORD for each space. You will hear the conversation twice.\nAnswer all the questions.\nLet's Plan and Save Now!\nBeing financially aware and managing money (21) ______  is important especially\nwhen preparing for college. While existing savings are helpful and is a good start, they will not cover\nall expenses like tuition, books, food, (22) ______  _ and emergency expenses. To avoid\nunnecessary financial stress, create a budget that will help to track income from allowances, (23) ______ or part-time jobs while managing expenses wisely. To start making a budget, keep\ntrack of expenses by using a budgeting app or spreadsheet to estimate (24) ______ expenses such as RM200 for food, RM100 for transport and setting aside the rest for savings. To\nsave money, take advantage of student (25) ______ and buy second-hand textbooks. In\naddition to that, there are plenty of ways to earn extra income while studying such as getting part-\ntime jobs, doing freelance (26) ______ work like graphic design, writing, data entry and\neven tutoring to earn money. Apart from making money while studying, a crucial part of financial\n(27) ______ is to save at least 20% of any income that you get. A good way to do this is\nthe 50/30/20 rule where we set aside 50% of the income for necessities, 30% for (28) ______ wants and 20% for savings. When we start saving early, even with small amounts,\nwe will build good habits and have emergency (29) ______ when needed. Being\nresponsible is not about limiting enjoyment but making smart (30) ______ in order to have\nfinancial freedom in the future."
  },
  "1d19Ah_TtEI-q6nHj3zi-VX_fxorC6Yef": {
    "1": "Part 1\nQuestions 1 - 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1-7, choose the\ncorrect answer A, B or C.\nYou will hear each recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n1. Why hasn't Ron seen Jane in a while?\nA Jane was working part time at the library.\nB Jane was busy doing her work.\nC Jane was hiding.\n2 What does the speaker enjoy the most at the market?\nA Talking to the vendors\nB Buying vegetables\nC Getting a snack\n3 Why is Aiman upset?\nA He lost his phone.\nB His phone was missing and broken.\nC His sister used his phone without permission.\n4 What should everyone do for a cleaner environment?\nA Practice home composting\nB Increase waste produced\nC Keep useable things\n5 How does the writer make such good characters? Through\nA personal emotions\nB people observation\nC inspiring story-telling\n6 The best feature of this app is it\nA is suitable for everyone\nB connects our friends\nC is a powerful device\n7 What does Lyra's Mum want her to do?\nA read all the brochures\nB choose her dream job\nC finalise her life goal",
    "2": "Part 2\nQuestions 8 - 15 '\nYou will hear Roy talk about his first experience travelling alone. For questions 8 -15, circle\nthe correct answer (A, B or C).\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n8 Roy was excited because\nA his friends called him brave.\nB he was going on his first solo trip.\nC he remembers clicking 'Confirm Booking'.\n9 Why did Roy want to travel alone?\nA He had finished college.\nB He wanted to be an adult.\nC He wanted a new experience.\n10 Describe Roy's feelings when he first stepped out of the plane.\nA Scared\nB Lonely\nC Confident\n11 How did Roy find his hostel?\nA He had screenshotted the directions.\nB A few locals communicated with him.\nC An old man at a fruit stall helped him.\n12 What did Roy do in the upcoming days?\nA He went sightseeing.\nB He was in a whirlwind.\nC He learned to drive the tuk-tuk.\n13 What was one of the challenges Roy faced?\nA He had to take a cooking class.\nB He had an upset stomach.\nC He had to take a bus.\n14 According to Roy, when you travel solo,\nA you communicate better with people.\nB you do everything on your own.\nC you won't get lost anymore.\n15 Why did Roy smile during the journey back home?\nA He had discovered himself.\nB He learned how to cook.\nC He enjoyed himself.",
    "3": "Part 3\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five short extracts in which people talk about the benefits of photography as a\nhobby. For questions 16 - 20, choose from the list, A- G, what each speaker says. Use the\nletters only once. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all questions.\nA It boosts my self-confidence.\nB Helps me learn new things.\nSpeaker 1 16\nC I can be creative.\nSpeaker 2 17\nD I am able to capture memories.\nSpeaker 3 18\nE Makes me feel relaxed.\nSpeaker 4 19\nF I organise events. Speaker 5 20\nG I have found a new job.",
    "4": "Part 4\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear a conversation between Sophia Tan, an influencer and a podcaster. For\nquestions 21 - 30, fill in the missing information in each numbered space. Use NO MORE\nTHAN ONE WORD for each space.\nYou will hear the interview twice. Answer all the questions.\nTete'-A-Tete' With An Influencer\nSophia Tan is an up and coming influencer who has made her mark in social media.\nShe has had an amazing (21) ______  until now and has all the makings to continue striving\nfor more. Her path was purely accidental. In fact, she was struggling with a trying (22) ______ in her life and started by sharing inspirational quotes in her social media posts. The\npositive feedback (23) ______ her to continue her work. After all, finding joy in the little\nthings in life not only inspired her but she found the need to spread the positivity. Her posts are\nvery much (24) ______ to her followers.\nThough it may be simpler to be trendy, Sophia feels it is much more important to be\noriginal. She sometimes receives (25) ______ responses and learns from them too. During\ntimes like this, she tries to recharge and goes (26) ______ to enjoy her hobbies. There are\nmany things she hopes to (27) ______  in her life. Other than spreading positivity in the lives\nof others, she also wants her followers to make their own way. She also only believes in\nworking only with (28) ______ that share her passion to not only help promote health but\nself-care as well. She believes a new (29) ______  should be patient and original to ensure\nsuccess. She has made meaningful connections where her content has echoed with regular\npeople. Her steady rise can be routed back to her (30) ______  even in this world where\neverything is needed fast and now.\n\n•"
  },
  "1H2QIYzGeh8mMfhS2f4YoDlD8m67bX3Rf": {
    "1": "Part 1\nQuestions 1 to 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the\ncorrect answer A, B or C.\nYou will hear each recording twice. Answer all questions.\n1. The person who had influenced Anis was her\nA. sister.\nB. mother.\nC. grandmother.\n2. Why hasn't Emma tried online shopping?\nA. She prefers to shop with her niece at the mall.\nB. She is afraid of being scammed like her niece.\nC. She could not find the dress she wanted online.\n3. The main reason Janet keeps on moving is because\nA. she does not have a permanent job.\nB. she prefers a small comfortable room.\nC. she gets tired climbing the apartment stairs.\n\n4. Hannah won the competition because of\nA. her clear and strong voice.\nB. her sad and emotional performance.\nC. her experience in singing in many competitions.\n5. The school was closed for two days due to\nA. The students were coughing and feeling unwell.\nB. There was a forest fire near the school.\nC. The air outside was too polluted.\n6. Why is the mother upset?\nA. He spent too much time playing games.\nB. He left his dirty clothes in the laundry basket.\nC. He forgot to do the chores she asked him to do.\n7. How does social media contribute to the spread of fake news?\nA. It shows only verified news.\nB. It often shows sensational news\nC. It encourages people to check facts.",
    "2": "Part 2\nQuestions 8 to 15\nYou will listen to a theatre actress, Diana, talking about her experience in theatre.\nFor questions 8 to 15, choose the best answer A, B or C.\n8. How did Diana first become interested in theatre?\nA. She went to performances with her parents.\nB. She joined a school play and fell in love with it.\nC. She was inspired by a famous production.\n9. What does Diana enjoy most about performing?\nA. The energy she gets from being in the room.\nB. The strong bond she created with the audience.\nC. The good feedback she received from the viewers.\n10. What does Diana find most difficult about working in the theatre?\nA. The physical demands of acting.\nB. The stress of memorising scripts.\nC. The tight schedule with rehearsals.\n11. What surprised Diana when she started acting professionally?\nA. The difficulty in getting her first role.\nB. The competitiveness in the theatre industry.\nC. The teamwork involved in theatre performance.\n\n12. How does Diana feel about stage fright?\nA. It makes her nervous during the performances.\nB. It gets worse as she performs on the stage.\nC. It disappears once she speaks her line.\n13. How does Diana prepare for a role?\nA. By studying the character's background.\nB. By rehearsing her lines in front of the mirror.\nC. By watching different adaptations of the same play.\n14. What does Diana say about working with different directors?\nA. It helps her grow because each has a unique style.\nB. It makes acting easier since directors guide actors.\nC. It is frustrating because each has different goals.\n15. What is Diana's future plan?\nA. Directing her own plays.\nB. Acting in movies instead of theatre.\nC. Teaching young actors in drama schools.",
    "3": "Part 3\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five speakers share their thoughts about financial awareness among\nteenagers. For questions 16 to 20, choose from the list A to G what each speaker says. Use\nthe letters only once. There are two extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all questions.\nA Having side jobs eases struggles and helps teenagers manage their expenses.\nB A limited financial understanding can lead to poor spending habits and debt.\nC Teenagers should learn how to balance their part-time jobs with their studies.\nD Understanding essentials and desires helps teenagers make better choices.\nE Teenagers need to understand the value of money to make wise financial decisions.\nF Teenagers should learn the best ways to spend money on non-essential items.\nG Proper budgeting and planning prepare teenagers for future responsibilities.\n16. Speaker 1\n17. Speaker 2\n18. Speaker 3\n19. Speaker 4\n20. Speaker 5",
    "4": "Part 4\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear an interview about urban playgrounds and how parkour has become a popular\nmovement sport in cities. For questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information in each\nnumbered space.\nUse NO MORE THAN ONE WORD OR A NUMBER for each space.\nYou will listen to the interview twice. Answer all questions.\nUrban Sports - Parkour\nParkour is a movement discipline that involves running, jumping, climbing, and\nvaulting over obstacles. The aim is to move (21) ______ and creatively through\nspace using only the body. Mr. Alex developed an interest in parkour after watching videos\nonline when he was a (22) ______. Then he started practising with friends, learning\nthe basics and eventually became a trainer.\nA parkour (23) ______ sees city structures differently from regular\npedestrians. Most people see a staircase as just a way to go up or down, parkour athletes\nsee it as a place to jump, vault, or practice footwork. Parkour can be\n(24) ______  , but safety is essential, so novices start with basic moves, learn how to\nland correctly, and train gradually. Parkour training can also improve strength and reduce the\nchance of (25) ______. Skills like problem-solving, creativity, and self-confidence\ncan also be taught through this sport. Unlike traditional sports, parkour does not require\n(26) ______  equipment. Instead, it uses everyday structures found in cities.\nParkour is changing the way people interact with urban spaces, and designing\ncities with parkour in mind could make movement more accessible to everyone. Some cities\nhave already started (27) ______  parkour parks, encouraging people to explore\nmovement in a safe environment. The key to start is to start slow and focus on balancing,\n(28) ______ and landing safely. Finding a coach or training group can be helpful,\nand it's important to respect your (29) ______. More than just a sport, parkour is a\nfun and (30) ______ way to move, allowing people to see their cities in a whole new\nway. With the right approach, parkour can be a safe and exciting way to stay active and\ncreative."
  },
  "1OcHlDLyhxfZevL3PYaY4uJnO-XwJB4es": {
    "1": "Part 1\n17 marks]\nQuestion 1 to 7\nYou will hear people talking in seven different situations. For questions 1 to 7, choose the\ncorrect answer (A, B or C). You will hear each recording twice. Answer all the questions.\n1. From the monologue, what made the speaker choose the book?\nA. The scent of the book.\nB. The content of the book.\nC. The recommendation of a friend.\n2. Based on the announcement, who are invited to come?\nA. Parents\nB. Teachers\nC. Senior students\n3. Based on the interview, how does the student study?\nA. By taking notes.\nB. By practicing every day.\nC. By managing her time well.\n4. How did the cashier solve the issue based on the conversation?\nA. Gave a discount.\nB. Offered a free item.\nC. Paid back the extra money.\n5. What does she complain about the play?\nA. The script.\nB. The length.\nC. The actors.\n6. Why has the flight EK001 been delayed?\nA. Technical issues.\nB. Late crew arrival.\nC. Bad weather conditions.\n7. What is the special offer on bread at Darling Supermarket?\nA. Buy two, get one free.\nB. Buy one, get two free.\nC. 50% off.",
    "2": "Part 2\n[8 marks]\nQuestion 8 to 15\nYou will hear a man talking about skateboarding. For questions 8 to 15, circle the correct answer\n(A, B or C). You will hear the recording twice. Answer all questions.\n8. How did the speaker feel while watching the teenagers skateboard?\nA. Confused by what they could do.\nB. Bored after a few minutes.\nC. Impressed by their skills.\n9. What happened after the speaker asked for a skateboard?\nA. The parents promised to buy it later.\nB. The parents agreed to the request right away.\nC. The parents refused and said it was too dangerous.\n10. What kept the speaker from giving up on skateboarding despite the difficulties?\nA. Feeling proud of every little improvement.\nB. Winning competitions and earning medals.\nC. Being the fastest skater in the park.\n11. How did the speaker describe the experience of learning to skateboard?\nA. As a way to achieve victory or stand out.\nB. As a difficult but rewarding journey.\nC. As an easy skill to master quickly.\n12. What life skills did the speaker gain from skateboarding?\nA. Time management, self-discipline and patience.\nB. Determination, patience and concentration.\nC. Problem-solving, focus and confidence.\n13. How did skateboarding affect the speaker's daily habits?\nA. It encouraged him to be outdoors and focus on learning tricks.\nB. It made him spend more time online and less time exercising.\nC. It led him to stop playing games completely.\n14. What makes the skateboarding community special to the speaker?\nA. Only experienced skaters are allowed to join.\nB. It is an environment where people appreciate each other.\nC. Everyone supports one another regardless of skill level.\n15. How does skateboarding help the speaker in daily life?\nA. It helps him improve his academic grades.\nB. It gives him more time to stay indoors and rest\nC. It gives him a way to relax and handle pressure.",
    "3": "Part 3\n15 marks]\nQuestions 16 to 20\nYou will hear five short extracts in which people talk about eco-friendly habits. For questions\n16 to 20, choose from the list (A to G) what each speaker says. Use the letters only once.\nThere are two extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\nA. Cycles or walks to school to cut down on air pollution and fuel consumption.\nB. Separating paper, packaging and kitchen waste makes it easier for children to help\nparents clean.\nC. Always buy eco-friendly containers even if they cost more than regular ones in stores.\nD. Saves energy by turning off lights and unplugging devices not in use\nE. Uses reusable containers to reduce single-use plastic and encourage others to do\nthe same.\nF. Uses solar panels at home to generate clean energy and save on electricity bills.\nG. Sorts household waste properly to make recycling easier and reduce garbage sent\nto landfills.\nSpeaker 1 16.\nSpeaker 2 17.\nSpeaker 3 18.\nSpeaker 4 19.\nSpeaker 5 20.",
    "4": "Part 4\n[10 marks]\nQuestions 21 to 30\nYou will hear an interview about Spotify. For questions 21 to 30, fill in the missing information\nin each numbered space. Use NO MORE THAN ONE WORD for each space.\nYou will hear the recording twice. Answer all the questions.\nAmin is a regular user of Spotify, a popular music streaming platform. He listens to\nmusic almost every day because it lifts his mood and helps him stay focused. He enjoys\nSpotify because it is easy to use, and he can quickly search for songs, albums and artists.\nSpotify offers (21) ______ of songs of different genres from all over the world.\nThere are two versions of Spotify. One version includes (22) ______ and only\na few song skips. However, Amin is planning to try Spotify Premium because it is ads free,\nhas unlimited skips and the ability to download songs for (23) ______ listening.\nHe also mentioned that Spotify offers different (24) ______ including student,\nindividual, duo and family plans. The student plan is (25) ______ and suitable for\nusers like him.\nOne of Amin's favourite features is \"Discover Weekly\", which gives him new songs\nevery Monday. He also enjoys \"Daily Mix\", where he gets playlists based on his music\n(26) ______ Amin's favourite artists include Ed Sheeran, BTS and Taylor Swift.\nHe creates his own playlists for studying, working out and relaxing and Spotify also allows\nhim to (27) ______ them with his friends.\nHe uses Spotify on different (28) ______ including his phone, laptop,\ntablet and smart TV. For Amin, Spotify is better than buying CDs because it is more\n(29) ______ and saves time. The only thing he doesn't like is the number of ads\non the free version, but he understands that it (30) ______ users to use the app at\nno cost.\nIn conclusion, Amin recommends Spotify for anyone who enjoys music anytime,\nanywhere."
  }
};
